{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PRE-EMPTIVE REMEDIATION before this ticket's first plan review: TWO acceptance criteria required their evidence in the GERRIT COMMIT MESSAGE. The completion verifier reads repository files ONLY and explicitly cannot read git history, so both were unconfirmable as written and would each have burned a close cycle — the same trap that blocked 7cbe's close and then reappeared on b300 and b5fe.\n\nSplit along the repo boundary rather than blanket-tagging:\n * AC3's LOC evidence — the IN-REPO half is now a TEST asserting runner.py < 700 and the new module clearing the 100-LOC floor. The commit-message half is [operator-attested], and it is worth keeping separately because the METHOD-body number is the one that has repeatedly been optimistic: ADR 0056 projected run() at 260, revised to 310, and MEASURED 323 after 7f86. A file-level LOC test cannot see that.\n * AC4's monkeypatch-repoint evidence — [operator-attested] for the message, with the observation that the in-repo half is what actually protects the suite: a repointed target must keep taking effect, which the full suite passing UNEDITED already demonstrates.\n\nfile_impact was already set on this ticket (3 paths), so no change there."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785577924294482001,"uuid":"2cf918c1-bca1-4f18-8ca0-588dc0dc1e6c"}